---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { DebtHolders } from '../../components/islands/DebtHolders'
import { DebtMaturity } from '../../components/islands/DebtMaturity'
import { debt, debtHolders, debtMaturity, curatedVintage, vintageOf } from '../../data'

// Sections built so far. Everything else in `sections` below falls through to
// the "Not built yet." placeholder until its own issue lands.
const built = new Set(['forty-trillion', 'who-holds-it', 'how-old'])

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T at the
      close of 18 August 2026, reported the following day, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion at the close of 18 August 2026, reported the following day."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-holds-it">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 2</span></p>
    <h2>Most of it is owed to us</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      About 80% is held by the public: investors, pension funds, banks, the Federal Reserve
      and foreign governments. The other 20% the government owes itself, mostly the Social
      Security and Medicare trust funds.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      $32.14T held by the public (80.6%), $7.74T intragovernmental (19.4%), as of 7 August
      2026.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="$32.14 trillion of the federal debt is held by the public and $7.74 trillion is held intragovernmentally by federal trust funds; about 30% of the publicly held part, 24% of gross, is held abroad."
      title="Who holds the federal debt, 7 August 2026"
      source={debtHolders._meta.source}
      vintage={curatedVintage(debtHolders._meta, debtHolders.data.as_of)}
      xUnit="Share of gross debt, and share of publicly held debt"
      yUnit="holder category"
      note="Foreign holders are about 30% of publicly held debt, which is about 24% of gross debt; the two are not interchangeable. Treasury International Capital is revised monthly and China has been reported between $683 billion and $760 billion depending on vintage."
    >
      <DebtHolders client:visible d={debtHolders.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Of the publicly held part, roughly 70% is domestic. The biggest foreign holders are
      Japan at about $1.20 trillion, the UK at $889 billion and China at $683 billion.
      Foreign holders were 5% of the debt in 1970 and are now close to a quarter of it. That
      is not a solvency problem by itself, but a growing share of interest payments leaves
      the country.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The intragovernmental piece is real money owed to future retirees, but it does not
      trade in bond markets and does not set interest rates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-old">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 3</span></p>
    <h2>The debt is younger than you think</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The longest security the Treasury sells is the 30-year bond, so nothing outstanding
      today was issued before 1996. But the average is nowhere near thirty years. It is
      about six.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Average maturity of marketable Treasury debt is 71 months as of June 2026. Roughly a
      third matures within twelve months.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Chart showing US marketable Treasury debt split by instrument type, with bills under one year, notes of two to ten years and bonds of twenty to thirty years, above a scale marking the average maturity at about six years against the thirty-year maximum."
      title="Maturity structure of marketable Treasury debt, June 2026"
      source={debtMaturity._meta.source}
      vintage={curatedVintage(debtMaturity._meta, debtMaturity.data.avg_maturity_as_of)}
      xUnit="Years to maturity"
      yUnit="amount outstanding, $ trillions"
      note="Bills, notes and bonds are the three instrument families and their amounts do not sum exactly to the $28.0 trillion marketable total; they are not presented as an exhaustive partition. The 22% bills figure is the curated share and is not derived from the amounts beside it. Savings bonds (Series EE and I) are non-marketable and excluded throughout."
    >
      <DebtMaturity client:visible d={debtMaturity.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      This is why the interest line moved so fast. The government does not lock in a rate
      for a generation. It refinances continuously, so when rates rose after 2021 the cost
      of the entire stock repriced within a few years rather than a few decades. See
      <a href="#net-interest">net interest</a> for what that did to the bill.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.filter((s) => !built.has(s.id)).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
